// Gorilla TPFanControl for Linux - settings (/etc/gorilla-fan.conf)
//
// Same key names as the Windows version's TPFanControl.ini, so a line can be
// copied from one to the other. Lines starting with // are comments. The fan
// service reads this file when it starts: after editing by hand, run
//   sudo systemctl restart gorilla-fan
// Choices made in the window are saved here by the service itself.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Mode at start: 1 BIOS, 2 Smart (the curve below), 3 Manual (ManFanSpeed),
// 4 Gorilla (cooling first, see below).
Active=2
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Manual mode level: 0-7 (0 stops the fan), or 0x40 for full speed.
ManFanSpeed=4
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Above this temperature (C): Manual switches back to Smart, and Gorilla
// runs full speed whatever its settings.
ManModeExit=78
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Seconds between decisions.
Cycle=5
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Sensors left out of the "hottest sensor" that Smart mode steers by
// (names as in the window: cpu aps crd gpu no5 x7d bat x7f bus pci pwr xc3).
IgnoreSensors=
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Gorilla mode (cooling first; noise is not a factor).
// Steers by the CPU sensor: full speed from GorillaTarget, level 7 within
// 5 C below it, otherwise GorillaFloor. Never slower than GorillaFloor.
// GorillaTarget: 30-70 C (the window offers 40, 45, 50).
// GorillaFloor: 1-7, or 64 (0x40, full speed all the time).
GorillaTarget=45
GorillaFloor=5
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Measured fan speed (rpm) of levels 0-7 and full speed, 9 numbers. Only
// used to label the level lists with % and rpm. The window's "Measure fan
// speeds" button fills it in. Example, ThinkPad L15 Gen 3:
// FanLevelRpm=0 1765 2179 2586 2790 2999 3197 3423 3445
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Smart mode curve: Level=temperature fan-level [hysteresis-up hysteresis-down]
// The fan takes the highest level whose temperature the hottest sensor has
// reached. Upstream TPFanControl's default curve:
Level=50 0
Level=60 1
Level=70 2
Level=80 4
Level=90 7
